Title: Enable JPEG XL by default
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Chromium 152 builds its JPEG XL decoder into every binary --
enable_jxl_decoder defaults to true in third_party/blink/renderer/config.gni,
and nothing in Electron's args turns it off -- but leaves it behind
blink::features::kJXLImageFormat, which is disabled by default. So the code is
shipped and paid for, and a .jxl image still fails to load unless the app
passes --enable-features=JXLImageFormat.

This flips the default. The one feature gates everything that makes the format
usable: the decoder's signature sniffing and creation in image_decoder.cc,
image/jxl as a supported MIME type, and image/jxl in the Accept header for
image and navigation requests, which is what lets a server choose to send it.

Nothing is added to the build, so there is no size or dependency change, and
--disable-features=JXLImageFormat turns it back off.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/common/features.cc b/third_party/blink/common/features.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/common/features.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/common/features.cc
@@ -1019,7 +1019,11 @@
                         &isolated_sandboxed_iframes_grouping_types);
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_JXL_DECODER)
-BASE_FEATURE(kJXLImageFormat, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+// On by default here. The decoder is compiled into every build already
+// (enable_jxl_decoder in renderer/config.gni); this is only the switch that
+// lets it be used, and it stays overridable with
+// --disable-features=JXLImageFormat.
+BASE_FEATURE(kJXLImageFormat, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 #endif
 
 BASE_FEATURE(kKeepAliveInBrowserMigration, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
